\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 ## Failure Mode
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 A platform team builds an avails API without normalizing the response format across media owners. Agency planners receive structured data they still cannot compare, so they export it to Excel and continue the manual consolidation. The platform reports "API adoption" while the actual workflow, and the revenue, continues to run on spreadsheets. Six months later, leadership concludes that agencies "resist automation," when in fact the automation never reached the decision point.
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The mirror-image failure: a team commits to real-time bidding for digital screens before digitizing insertion orders and proof of posting. Programmatic revenue arrives on top of an unreconciled billing base, and every programmatic buy inherits the manual workflow's discrepancy rate. The advertiser applies a discount to all future buys.
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 ## Translation Conversation: Product and Engineering
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 In 60 seconds: "This market cannot do real-time bidding on most of its inventory, because most of its inventory is vinyl. Our job is to digitize the workflow where the revenue already lives: avails, rate cards, IOs, proof of posting. Then we connect the digital minority of screens to programmatic demand through guaranteed deals, not open auctions. The order matters. Automating the wrong layer first means we spend a year building an auction nobody can use while the billing discrepancies keep eating the margin."
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 ## Open-Source Implementation
\f2\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The reconciliation pattern above connects to the data quality and lineage work in the portfolio repositories: payload mismatch auditing across system boundaries (see Ad Tech SQL Analysis) is the same failure class as IO-to-proof-of-posting reconciliation, with an invoice attached. The pricing rules table is the direct antecedent of the floor-price logic in BidOptimizerPro; a negotiated rate card and a programmatic floor are the same control at different points in a market's maturity.
